 apply: {
  vignette: "A 38-year-old with stage III periodontitis smokes 20 cigarettes a day. Asked about quitting, he says he enjoys smoking and is not planning to stop. Which of the following is the most appropriate approach at this visit?",
  options: ["Relate smoking to his own gum disease and ask what he thinks about that link","Ask him to set a quit date within the next two weeks","Prescribe nicotine patches and arrange a follow-up call next week","Tell him firmly that periodontal treatment will fail unless he quits"],
  answer: "Relate smoking to his own gum disease and ask what he thinks about that link",
  steps: [
   "Step 1: the ask is the most appropriate approach for this patient's stage of change.",
   "Step 2: he is not considering quitting, which is precontemplation.",
   "Step 3: eliminate the quit date and the patches. Both are tools for someone in preparation, and pushing them now meets resistance.",
   "Step 4: eliminate the warning. Confrontation tends to strengthen resistance and closes the conversation.",
   "Step 5: the survivor personalises the risk to his own mouth and invites his view, which is how motivational interviewing builds discrepancy and moves a patient towards contemplation."
  ]
 },
 teach: {
  prompt: "Explain to a colleague why handing a reluctant smoker a pamphlet and a warning is less effective than the approach you would use, and what you would actually say.",
  model: "A warning tells him what he already knows and puts him in the position of defending his habit, which hardens resistance. Motivational interviewing works the other way: I ask open questions, reflect what he says, and link smoking to something he cares about, such as his bleeding gums or keeping his teeth. When he voices his own reasons to change, he is more likely to act. I match the step to his stage: for now, awareness and an open door, and later a quit date, medication and a quitline. I record his status and ask again at every visit, because brief repeated advice adds up."
 },
